&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fighting for a Foothold: How Government and Markets Undermine Black Middle-Class Suburbia Fighting for a Foothold&lt;/em&gt; argues that Black middle-class counties subsidize White middle-class counties&amp;rsquo; tax revenue generation due to the unique constraints Black jurisdictions face&amp;mdash;the cumulative effects of anti-Black racism, raced and classed flows of people and capital into local jurisdictions, and shared authority across levels of government that fails to account for Black jurisdictions&amp;rsquo; historical and ongoing disadvantages.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Angela Simms&lt;/strong&gt; is Assistant Professor of Sociology and Urban Studies at Barnard College, Columbia University. She examines the political economy of United States metropolitan areas through the lens of suburban Black middle-class jurisdictions&amp;rsquo; capacity to garner sufficient tax revenue for maintaining high-quality public goods and services. On February 24, 2026, she will publish the book Fighting for a Foothold: How Government and Markets Undermine Black Middle-Class Suburbia, through the Russell Sage Foundation. She has also published articles related to local government financial capacity, including &amp;ldquo;Fiscal Fragility in Black Middle-Class Suburbia and Consequences for K-12 Public Schools and Other Public Services,&amp;rdquo; in The Russell Sage Foundation Journal of the Social Sciences (2023); and &amp;ldquo;COVID-19, Black Jurisdictions, and Budget Constraints: How Fiscal Footing Shapes Fighting the Virus,&amp;rdquo; in Racial and Ethnic Studies (2021).&amp;nbsp; Angela serves as a co-chair of Columbia Population Research Center&amp;rsquo;s Working Group on Urbanism and Neighborhoods. She is also on the editorial board of Sociology of Race and Ethnicity Journal. During the 2023-2024 academic year, Angela was a Russell Sage Foundation Visiting Scholar.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

