Tel Hashomer, Ramat Gan, 5262000, Israel&lt;br /&gt;
Chair of&amp;nbsp;Academy of&amp;nbsp;the International Association for the Study of Lung Cancer (IASLC)&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
Full Prof. of Oncology (regular track),&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
The Gray Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences,&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
Tel Aviv University,&amp;nbsp;Tel Aviv, Israel&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;quot;The role of B-cells in immune checkpoint inhibitor efficacy in lung cancer&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description&lt;/strong&gt;:&amp;nbsp;Immune-check-point inhibitors (ICI) have transformed the treatment of lung cancer patients, achieving higher response rates and durable responses lasting years in some cases. However, the majority of patients demonstrate primary or secondary resistance to these drugs. Insight into the mechanisms of action of ICI and into resistance mechanisms is required in order to improve on these results. Our lab, as well as other labs have identified B cells and plasma cells as major participants in the response to ICI. Spatial proteomics of NSCLC specimens and additional studies demonstrate early activated B cells, as well as activated memory T-cells to be involved in this response, mostly within tertiary lymphoid structures. Using single-cell RNA and B cell receptor sequencing we have identified clonal expansion of specific B-cell subtypes. To conclude, the response to ICI involves coordinated T-cell as well as B-cell activation, suggesting novel therapeutic targets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hosted by&amp;nbsp;Dr. Brian Henick.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.auburn.edu/cosam/faculty/math_stats/lin/index.htm" target="_blank"&gt;Junshan Lin&lt;/a&gt;, Auburn University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Resonances through Subwavelength Holes: Theory, Computation and Applications&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The so-called extraordinary optical transmission (EOT) through metallic nanoholes has triggered extensive research in modern plasmonics and their applications in bio-sensing, imaging, etc. In this talk, I will give an overview of quantitative mathematical theory to understand a variety of resonances that induce the EOT phenomenon in 2D and 3D subwavelength structures, the computational methods for solving the multiscale problems, and the mathematical studies for their applications in imaging and sensing.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Junshan Lin is currently the C Harry Knowles Endowed Professor of Mathematics at Auburn University. His research lies in Applied and Computational Mathematics, especially in Mathematical Problems for Waves in Novel Materials, Inverse Wave Scattering and Imaging, Computational Inverse and Optimal Design.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;In person attendance at this seminar is only open to Columbia University affiliates. External guests are welcome to attend remotely. Please contact &lt;a href="mailto:apam@columbia.edu?subject=Zoom%20link%20needed%20for%20AM%20Colloquium" target="_blank"&gt;apam@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; if you need the Zoom link for this seminar. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

